<time datetime="2023-04-04">April 4, 2023</time>
<article id="DNS" class="WebBits">
    <h2>LEARN: DNS</h2>
    <p>
        <strong>Domain Name System</strong>,
        <abbr title="Domain Name System">DNS</abbr>, empowers computers to
        find things on the internet. That is a big concept. A dns server will
        tell a client what <abbr title="Internet Protocol">IP</abbr> address
        matches the domain request.
    </p>
    <aside class="exampleBlue">
        <p>
            Dns operates on port 53 via
            <abbr title="User Datagram Protocol">UDP</abbr> for client queries
            or <abbr title="Transmission Control Protocol">TCP</abbr> for zone
            transfers.
        </p>
    </aside>
    <p>
        Domain <strong>registrars</strong> use
        <strong>records</strong> &lpar;A/AAAA/CNAME/MX/others&rpar; to tell
        <strong>nameservers</strong> that a domain exists. Without a record of
        existence, a domain/website can only be reached from the network it is
        hosted within. Dns works in a hierarchical fashion utilizing cached
        entries to find sites from the internet's dns
        <strong>root servers</strong>.<sup>1</sup>
    </p>
    <hr class="dl">
    <dl>
        <dt>Domain Name System</dt>
        <dd>
            DNS is a resolution method associating a domain name to an IP
            address.
        </dd>
        <dt>DNS Records</dt>
        <dd>
            DNS records are Resource Records &lpar;RRs&rpar; that map the domain
            to things like IPv4, IPv6 or an alias &lpar;CNAME&rpar;.
        </dd>
        <dt>Nameservers</dt>
        <dd>
            A domain's DNS server which communicates with resolvers from a
            domain.
        </dd>
        <dt>Registrars</dt>
        <dd>
            Domain registrars are places where domains can be bought and
            managed, like GoDaddy, NameCheap, or Google Domains.
        </dd>
        <dt>Resolvers</dt>
        <dd>Internet operating nameservers in contact with root servers.</dd>
        <dt>Root Servers</dt>
        <dd>13 root servers serve DNS content globally.</dd>
    </dl>
    <hr class="dl dictionaryWidget">
    <aside class="examplePink">
        <p>Common dns <strong>resolvers</strong> are:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><code>1.1.1.1</code> - &lpar;Cloudflare&rpar;</li>
            <li><code>8.8.8.8</code> - &lpar;Google&rpar;</li>
            <li><code>8.8.4.4</code> - &lpar;Google&rpar;</li>
        </ul>
    </aside>
    <p class="citations">
        <strong>Citations:</strong><br>
        <sup>[1]</sup>
        <cite>
            <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/learning/dns/dns-server-types/" target="_blank" rel="external noopener"
               title="Cloudflare: DNS server types">
                <q>DNS server types | Cloudflare</q>
            </a>
        </cite>
    </p>
</article>
